# Fish on a Friday - a chip shop, oil due to be changed, and an option that only appears if you did it.
# Written for the library. The next step past state changing a mid-story scene: here the state changes WHICH OPTIONS
# THE READER IS OFFERED, so an earlier decision alters the menu rather than only the colour of the prose.

title: Fish on a Friday
start: start

== start ==
bg workshop_bench
music room_tone
The haddock goes in and the phone rings at the same moment, which is how Friday starts, and you are
holding the basket with your shoulder up against the hood and the call is the man from the wholesaler
saying the delivery will be an hour late.
You have been frying in this shop for twenty-two years. You know what an hour late means on a Friday
the way you know the sound of the door.
-> the_oil

== the_oil ==
Which brings you to the oil, because everything on a Friday brings you to the oil.
It went in ten days ago and it is due out today, and you have been saying since Wednesday that you
will do it before the lunch, and it is now eleven o'clock and it is not done, and the oil in the
second fryer is dark at the edges of the pan and it smells of the thing it does when it is done.
Doing it now costs you the lunch and about four hundred pounds between the oil and the lost trade.
Doing it Monday costs you a Friday and a Saturday on oil that has gone.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
The shop is shut until twelve either way, and the fish is an hour late either way, and there is
nothing else to do with the next hour except decide this.
* Change it now. Lose the lunch, fry the evening on clean oil. -> change_it
* Run it to Monday. Four hundred pounds is four hundred pounds. -> run_it
* Change the second fryer only, and cook the haddock in the first. -> half_of_it

== change_it ==
You pump it out into the drum at the back and you put the new oil in and it takes fifty minutes
including the cleaning, and you open at twenty past twelve with the shop smelling of nothing, which
is the smell of clean oil and is the best smell a fryer has.
You do not get the lunch trade. You get the afternoon, and the afternoon on a Friday is coffee and
one pensioner with a small cod, and you stand behind the counter reading the paper.
~ fresh_oil 1
-> friday_evening

== run_it ==
You decide it will do Monday, which is what you decided on Wednesday, and which is the third time
this year.
You open at twelve on the same oil and it is fine for the lunch, and it is fine for the afternoon,
and by six o'clock the fish is coming out a shade darker than the fish you would put in front of
somebody you liked, and you are turning the second fryer down to keep it from going further, which
works and which you have done before.
-> friday_evening

== half_of_it ==
You change the second fryer and leave the first, which is the decision that sounds like a
compromise and is not: the second fryer is the one that does the chips, and the fish goes in the
first, and the first is the one that has gone.
You know this. You do it anyway, because it is eleven o'clock and the drum is heavy and doing half
of a job is a thing a tired man can talk himself into.
~ half 1
-> friday_evening

== friday_evening ==
if fresh_oil >= 1 -> the_clean_evening
if half >= 1 -> the_half_evening
-> the_dark_evening

== the_clean_evening ==
The evening is a proper Friday: forty-two suppers between half past five and half past eight, and
every one of them comes out the colour it should be, and the batter sits up on the fish instead of
sliding off it, and Mrs Okonjo says so, because she says so every year after you change the oil and
never notices herself doing it.
You are on your feet for three hours and your wrist is what it is and you would not be anywhere
else, and at half past nine you sit down on the crate by the door with a cup of tea and the door
open on the street.
-> the_late

== the_half_evening ==
The chips are right and the fish is not, and the evening runs on the split: the chips go out crisp
and pale and the fish goes out a shade dark, and about four people notice and none of them says
anything, and you know all four of them by name.
At ten o'clock you take the first fryer down and pump it into the drum in the dark at the back of
the shop, and you do it properly, which takes forty minutes, and you are home at eleven.
-> the_late

== the_dark_evening ==
The evening runs heavy. The fish goes dark early and stays dark, and by seven you are double
draining everything and running the extractor on full, and the shop smells of the oil rather than
of the fish, and two people who have been coming for years come in and look at the tray and order
chips.
At half past eight you make the decision you should have made at eleven, which is to stop frying,
and you tell the last three customers that you have run out of fish, which is not true, and they go
away fine with it because it is a Friday and they are used to things running out.
-> the_late

== the_late ==
-> the_choice

== the_choice ==
It is twenty to nine and there is a tray of fish under the lamp that you would not eat and a queue
of three, and the decision is not about the fish any more, it is about what the rest of the night
is for.
* Tell them the truth, shut the fryer, and put the reason in the window for tomorrow. -> the_truth
* Keep going to closing time and take the money. -> keep_going
* Take the fish off and sell chips until nine. -> chips_only
* [if !fresh_oil] Do the night properly: shut at nine, pump it out, and change it before you go home. -> the_night

== the_truth ==
You tell the three of them, who did not ask, that the fish is not good tonight and that you are not
selling it, and one of them says fair enough and one of them says it is only a bit of colour, and
the third one - who has been coming here since before you had the shop - says nothing at all and
comes back on the Tuesday.
You write it on a sheet of A4 and tape it inside the window: SORRY, NO FISH TONIGHT. BACK FRIDAY.
And it is the first time in twenty-two years that a thing has gone in that window that was not
either a price or a holiday.
-> END

== keep_going ==
You keep going. You sell the tray, and then you fry a fresh basket and sell that too, because a
Friday is a Friday and the queue does not care what you think of the oil.
At ten past ten you take eighty pounds out of the till and you stand in the back with the door open
and the extractor off and the smell of the whole evening on you, and you work out, because you
cannot help working it out, that the oil would have cost you four hundred and that you have made
one hundred and sixty of it back and lost a day of your life, and you go home and do not say any of
that to anybody.
-> END

== chips_only ==
You take the fish off the tray and put it in the bin, which is four pounds of haddock, and you sell
chips until nine, and it is a busy ninety minutes and everybody is happy and two people ask when
the fish is coming back.
You say Friday, and it is Friday, because you do not open on the Monday and the oil is changed on
the Saturday morning with the shop shut and nobody watching, and by the time anybody notices
anything the tray is full of haddock the colour it should be, and the three customers who wondered
on the Friday have long since stopped wondering.
-> END

== the_night ==
You shut at nine, which costs you an hour, and you pump the oil out into the drum and clean the pans
and put the new oil in, and it is twenty past eleven when you put the lid back on the drum and stand
up straight.
The shop is clean and the fryers are clean and tomorrow's oil is in, and you are fifty-four and your
back is what it is, and what you have bought with the last two hours is that nobody ate anything
tonight that you knew was not right - WHICH IS THE SAME THING YOU COULD HAVE BOUGHT AT ELEVEN THIS
MORNING FOR FOUR HUNDRED POUNDS AND AN HOUR, and you paid for it with a Friday instead, which you
think about on the way home and do not resolve.
-> END
